---
import WikiHowLayout from '../../layouts/WikiHowLayout.astro';

const title = "How to Print a Perfectly Accurate Protractor at Home";
const author = "Alex Chen";
const updatedAt = "July 22, 2026";
const views = "25,109";
const category = "Resources";
---
<WikiHowLayout title={title} author={author} updatedAt={updatedAt} views={views} category={category}>
  
  <p>Printer scaling issues can completely distort the mathematical geometry of a printable tool. Learn the exact settings required to print a mathematically perfect protractor from your home printer.</p>
  
  <img src="/images/blog/printing-protractor.png" alt="Printing a protractor" class="wikihow-image" />

  <h2><span class="wikihow-step-num">1</span> Disable Printer Scaling.</h2>
  <p>This is the most important step. When you open your print dialogue box, printers default to "Fit to Page" or "Scale to Fit" to ensure the image doesn't get cut off. This will ruin the protractor.</p>
  <p>You must change your scaling setting to <strong>"Actual Size"</strong> or <strong>"100% Scale"</strong>.</p>

  <h2><span class="wikihow-step-num">2</span> Download a Vector File.</h2>
  <p>Never print a JPG or PNG. Raster images can be blurry and distort easily. Always use a Vector format (SVG) or a high-quality PDF. Our <a href="/printable-protractors" class="text-[#9eb96e] font-bold hover:underline">Printable Protractor Library</a> uses pure SVGs.</p>

  <h2><span class="wikihow-step-num">3</span> Use the Right Paper.</h2>
  <p>Standard 20lb copy paper is too flimsy for a measurement tool.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Print on <strong>Cardstock (65lb - 110lb)</strong> for rigidity.</li>
    <li>Alternatively, print on standard paper and use a glue stick to adhere it to a piece of cardboard before cutting it out.</li>
    <li><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Print on transparent acetate sheets (transparency film) so you can see through it just like a real plastic protractor!</li>
  </ul>

  <h2><span class="wikihow-step-num">4</span> Test the Accuracy.</h2>
  <p>Before using it for important work, draw a straight line. Use your printed tool to mark exactly 90 degrees. Verify that mark using a known square edge (like the corner of a hardcover book). If it matches perfectly, your print settings were correct.</p>

</WikiHowLayout>
